      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-pan-79ee30c8ab8040e4\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The highlighted “jeg elsker dig” is principally a direct avowal that the narrator’s love for Eva is increasing. Its placement within talk of Eva accompanying him gives it slight courtship or relational-undertaking force, but the clause itself makes no clear commitment. Although delivered during an emotional embrace and after an exclamation, it remains adequately represented as an intense T-avowal; there is no independent evidence that these particular words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The narrator’s persistent preoccupation with Edvarda makes the sincerity and object of his feelings unstable, but this does not alter the utterance’s predominantly truth-conditional mode. Slight P support depends on its integration into the accompanying invitation to a shared future.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"The first-person narrator directly says “jeg elsker dig mere og mere” to Eva during their private conversation and embrace.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal follows a conversation repeatedly exposing the narrator’s fixation on Edvarda, including his implausible claim that calling Edvarda’s name really meant Eva. Eva’s kindness then moves him, and he declares increasing love while pressing her to him and proposing that she accompany him when he leaves. Thus the words function as an emotionally intense avowal and part of a courtship sequence, while the surrounding narrative casts doubt on his emotional clarity and possibly his sincerity toward Eva.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg er sikker på at jeg forgår av kjærlighet til dig, jeg elsker dig mere og mere” (“I am certain I shall perish from love for you; I love you more and more”).\",\"supports\":\"Strong T: the speaker explicitly presents an increasing state of love for Eva as true.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The declaration continues with “tilslut blir du med mig når jeg reiser … Kunde du følge med mig” (“in the end you will come with me when I leave … Could you come with me?”).\",\"supports\":\"Slight P: the avowal participates in an invitation toward a shared future, though it does not itself clearly undertake an obligation.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, Eva gently strokes his hair; he exclaims “Du gode, gode sjæl!” and presses her fiercely to him.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes intense and affectionate delivery, but the explicitly marked exclamation is the preceding address rather than clear evidence that the highlighted love clause itself has independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Throughout the conversation he repeatedly returns to Edvarda, admits having thought more about her, and says he called Edvarda’s name during the night while claiming that he meant Eva.\",\"supports\":\"Creates contextual doubt about sincerity, self-knowledge, and the true object of his attachment without changing the highlighted utterance’s avowal mode.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a present-tense, affirmative explicit love construction in Knut Hamsun’s Pan.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the highlighted form as an explicit first-person love predication addressed to another person.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core force: a strong avowal with weak context-dependent relational-undertaking pressure and no independently established reflexive discharge. The narrator’s divided attachment and possible manipulation belong to contextual interpretation rather than requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
